=== After partition ===
Apart from [[Punjab, India|Punjab]], Khatris arrived in [[Delhi]] and [[Haryana]] among other regions after the partition where they make up 9% and 8.0% of the population respectively.<ref name="mw"/><ref name="gh">{{Cite web|title=Report of Haryana Backward Classes Commission - 2012 {{!}} Welfare of Scheduled Caste & Backward Classes Department, Government of Haryana|url=http://haryanascbc.gov.in/report-of-haryana-backward-classes-commission-2012|access-date=29 August 2021|website=haryanascbc.gov.in}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Kumar |first=Sanjay |title=A tale of three cities |url=http://www.india-seminar.com/2004/534/534%20sanjay%20kumar.htm |website=India Seminar}}</ref>

== Clan organisation ==
Historically, Khatris were divided into various hierarchal [[Endogamy|endogamous]] sections. This includes [[Dhai Ghar|u''rhai/dhai ghar'']], ''char ghar'', [[Bahri|''barah ghar/bahri'']] and ''bunjayee or bavanjah ghar'' which translated to House of 2.5, 4, 12 and 52 respectively. They formed the majority of Khatris and were deemed superior. This was followed by [[Sareen]] Khatris who formed a minority. Another sub-group of Khatris include [[Khukhrain]] which had split up from the ''bunjayees''.<ref name="Oldenburg 2002 41, 15422">{{Cite book |last=Oldenburg |first=Veena Talwar |author-link=Veena Talwar Oldenburg |title=Dowry Murder: The Imperial Origins of a Cultural Crime |publisher=Oxford University Press |year=2002 |pages=41, 154}}</ref>
