== Varna status ==
{{disputed section|reason= Juergensmayer has summarised opinions of historians that the Kshatriya claim is generally not acccepted hence this looks like original research or POV. Wikipedia editors cannot form their own summary by synthesising sources.|date=May 2022}}

[[Mysore Narasimhachar Srinivas|M. N. Srinivas]] states Khatri made different Varna claims at different times in the [[Census of India prior to independence|Census of India]] before Independence. In 1911, they did not make any [[Varna (Hinduism)|Varna]] claim, while in 1921 they claimed a [[Kshatriya]] status but later in 1931 they claimed a [[Vaishya]] status.<ref>{{cite book|author=Mysore Narasimhachar Srinivas|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=MBOb5X_VrDMC&pg=PA97 |title=Social Change in Modern India|publisher=University of California Press-Berkeley and Los Angeles|year=1967|page=97}}</ref> However, most scholars do not agree with the Khatris' claims to Kshatriya varna. They consider castes in north India, like Khatri and [[Kayastha]] to be merchant castes who claimed higher status based on the educational and economic progress they made in the past.<ref>{{cite book|author=Mark Juergensmayer|chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=rpSxJg_ehnIC&pg=PA86|title=Bhakti Religion in North India: Community Identity and Political Action|date=1 January 1995|publisher=SUNY Press|isbn=978-0-7914-2025-6|editor=David N. Lorenzen|page=86|chapter=The social significance of Radhasoami|quote=In the past members of such castes such as Khatris served as shopkeepers, moneylenders, traders and teachers. Their reputation for mastering knowledge sometimes extended to the spiritual realm:Guru Nanak and the other nine founding gurus of the sikh tradition were Khatris, member of the Bedi subcaste.}}</ref>

According to [[Anand Yang|Yang]], the Khatris in the [[Saran district|Saran]] district of [[Bihar]], were included in the list of "Bania" along with Agarwals and Rastogis of the [[Vaishya]] Varna. According to Yang, their position in the Varna system should be "just below" the [[Dvija|twice-born]] varnas.<ref>{{cite book|author=Anand A. Yang|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ck4jmD7H34UC&pg=PA47|title=The Limited Raj: Agrarian Relations in Colonial India, Saran District, 1793-1920|publisher=University of California Press|year=1989|isbn=0520057112|access-date=22 September 2020}}</ref> Jacob Copeman writes "[[Agarwal]], Khatri, and Bania usually denote people of merchant-trader background of middling clean-caste status, often of Vaishya varna".<ref name="Copeman20092" />

While some historians agree with the claim of Khatris to be of [[Kshatriya]] varna,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Westerlund |first=David |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=hg2ZyH7nr9cC&dq=questioning+the+secular+state+punjabi+khatris+kshatriyas&pg=PA261 |title=Questioning the Secular State: The Worldwide Resurgence of Religion in Politics|date=1996|publisher=C. Hurst & Co. Publishers|isbn=978-1-85065-241-0|pages=261|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Lorenzen|first=David N.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=oa0qAQAAMAAJ&q=caste+system+khatris|title=Religious Movements in South Asia 600-1800|date=2005|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-567876-5|pages=321|language=en}}</ref><ref name="js">{{Cite book |last=Mooney |first=Nicola |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=3Hu4X3AOhS8C&q=khatris |title=Rural Nostalgias and Transnational Dreams: Identity and Modernity Among Jat Sikhs |date=17 September 2011|publisher=University of Toronto Press |isbn=978-1-4426-6268-1 |pages=152–153}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Clarke |first1=Peter B. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=k8mLAgAAQBAJ&dq=khatri+caste+warrior&pg=PT981|title=The World's Religions: Continuities and Transformations|last2=Beyer|first2=Peter|date=7 May 2009|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-1-135-21099-1|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Melton |first1=J. Gordon|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=v2yiyLLOj88C&dq=born+in+a+khatri+family+nanak&pg=PA2013|title=Religions of the World: A Comprehensive Encyclopedia of Beliefs and Practices, 2nd Edition [6 volumes]|last2=Baumann|first2=Martin|date=21 September 2010|publisher=ABC-CLIO |isbn=978-1-59884-204-3|pages=2013|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|author=Kamala Elizabeth Nayar|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_oPeWqdoqJQC&q=born+in+a+khatri+family&pg=PA214|title=Religious Understandings of a Good Death in Hospice Palliative Care|last2=Harold Coward|date=13 June 2012 |publisher=SUNY Press|isbn=978-1-4384-4275-4|editor-last=Kelli I. Stajduhar|pages=214|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Rai |first1=Rajesh |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YzkrDwAAQBAJ&dq=khatris+warrior&pg=PA104|title=Religion and Identity in the South Asian Diaspora |last2=Sankaran |first2=Chitra|date=5 July 2017|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-1-351-55159-5|pages=104|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Renard |first=John |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=fCjVRqPD-HQC&dq=ravi+das+khatri&pg=PA18|title=Fighting Words: Religion, Violence, and the Interpretation of Sacred Texts|date=31 December 2012|publisher=University of California Press|isbn=978-0-520-95408-3|pages=18|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Singh |first=Nikky-Guninder Kaur|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=k0s2dUUMHbkC&dq=khatri&pg=PA22|title=Sikhism|date=2004|publisher=Infobase Publishing |isbn=978-1-4381-1779-9|pages=22|language=en}}</ref> many others do not.<ref name="Lorenzen199522" /><ref>{{cite book |author=Anand A. Yang |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ck4jmD7H34UC&pg=PA47 |title=The Limited Raj: Agrarian Relations in Colonial India, Saran District, 1793-1920 |publisher=University of California Press |year=1989 |isbn=0520057112 |access-date=22 September 2020}}</ref><ref name="Copeman20092">{{cite book |author=Jacob Copeman |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=EbSXa5Fag9gC&pg=PA203 |title=Veins of Devotion: Blood Donation and Religious Experience in North India |publisher=Rutgers University Press |year=2009 |isbn=978-0-8135-4449-6 |pages=203 |quote=Agarwal, khatri, and bania usually denote people of merchant-trader background of middling clean-caste status, often of vaishya varna}}</ref><ref name="ma">{{cite book|author=Susan Bayly|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HbAjKR_iHogC&pg=PA329|title=Caste, Society and Politics in India from the Eighteenth Century to the Modern Age|publisher=Cambridge University Press|year=2001|isbn=9780521798426|pages=328–329|quote=Examples of continuing fascination with the Kshatriya ideal abound, as can be seen in the many post-Independence publications which exalt the doings of individual named jatis. The production of these ‘community’ histories has been as active an industry in the late twentieth century as it was in the pre-Independence period. As recently as 1988, a polemicist representing himself as an Oxford-trained Indian ‘socio-historian’ published an account of the supposed origins and heritage of north India's Khatris. Today, as in the past, those who call themselves Khatri favour the livelihoods of the pen and the ledger. In the colonial period, however, Khatri caste associations extolled the heritage of their ‘community’ as one of prowess and noble service (seva), claiming that their dharmic essence was that of the arms-bearing Kshatriya and therefore quite unlike that of the commercial Agarwals and other pacific Vaishyas. These same themes were recapitulated by the author of the 1988 text: the Khatris, ‘one of the most acute, energetic, and remarkable race [sic] in India’, are heirs to a glorious martial past, ‘pure descendants of the old Vedic Kshatriyas’. The writer even tries to exalt Khatris above Rajputs, whose blood he considers ‘impure’, being supposedly mixed with that of ‘inferior’ Kols or ‘aborigines’: in his view only Khatris are ‘true representatives of the Aryan nobility’.<39>Footnote: 39 Puri 1988: 3, 78, 163, 166. The writer appeals to the Khatri ‘race’ to ‘wake up’ and cherish their heritage as ‘followers of the Hindu Dharma Sastras’ (5). Above all they should guard against ‘hybridising’, i.e. marrying non-Khatris (166). These views closely resemble those of pre-Independence race theorists (see Chapters 3-4). Compare Seth 1904}}</ref><ref name="JonesJones19762">{{cite book|author1=Kenneth W. Jones |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=RpvXCtNzrz8C&pg=PA4|title=Arya Dharm: Hindu Consciousness in 19th-century Punjab|author2=Kenneth W.Jones |publisher=University of California Press|year=1976|isbn=978-0-520-02920-0|pages=4–5|quote=Among Punjabi Hindus the Vaishyas would lead; among Vaishyas, the Khatri and his associates, the Saraswat Brahmins. The Khatris claimed with some justice and increasing insistence, the status of Rajputs, or Kshatriyas, a claim not granted by British but illustrative of their ambiguous position on the great varna scale of class divisions and their importance within the Hindu community. Processed of questionable and flexible status in the traditional hierarchy, literate, urban and often wealthy, in search of recognition for their achievements and pretentions, the Khatris acted as traditional innovators, leaders into new worlds}}</ref><ref name="McLane2002222">{{cite book|last=McLane|first=John R.|title=Land and Local Kingship in Eighteenth-Century Bengal|publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2002 |isbn=978-0-521-52654-8|page=131|quote=The Khatris were a Punjabi mercantile caste who claimed to be Kshatriyas. Nineteenth-century Indians and British administrators failed to agree whether that claim should be accepted. The fact that overwhelming majority were engaged in Vaishya (mercantile), not Kshatriya (military), pursuits was balanced against the Khatri origin myths...By the eighteenth century, and probably long before, they were a dominant group in the trade of the Punjab and Afghanistan, and they had penetrated into Turkistan and also east and south into many parts of India. ...This raises the possibility that Khatris were resident in Bengal in pre-Mughal times.}}</ref> According to some historians, even though they participated in mercantile or other occupationally diverse professions such as Agriculture, they were originally Kshatriyas.<ref name="ca"/><ref>{{Cite book|last=Farhadian|first=Charles E.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5VV-BAAAQBAJ&q=khatri+sanskrit+punjabi&pg=PT293|title=Introducing World Religions: A Christian Engagement|date=9 June 2015|publisher=Baker Academic|isbn=978-1-4412-4650-9|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Jeffrey |first=Robin|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qQO_DAAAQBAJ&dq=khatris+warrior&pg=PA52|title=What's Happening to India?: Punjab, Ethnic Conflict, and the Test for Federalism|date=27 July 2016|publisher=Springer|isbn=978-1-349-23410-3|pages=52|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Pechilis |first1=Karen|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=kaubzRxh-U0C&dq=khatris+warrior&pg=PA239|title=South Asian Religions: Tradition and Today |last2=Singh |first2=Pashaura|last3=Raj|first3=Selva J.|date=2013|publisher=Routledge|isbn=978-0-415-44851-2|pages=239|language=en}}</ref><ref name="Oldenburg 2002 41, 15422" /> In Indian historian [[Satish Chandra (historian)|Satish Chandra]]'s opinion, certain castes like Khatris and Kayasthas "do not quite fit" in the Hindu Varna system. According to him, Khatris are neither Vaishyas nor Kshatriyas but are "par excellence traders".<ref name="Chandra20082">{{cite book |author=Satish Chandra|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=i6xVm9lkknUC|title=Social Change and Development in Medieval Indian History|publisher=Har-Anand Publications|year=2008|isbn=978-81-241-1386-8|pages=43|quote=In fact, there are some castes which do not quite fit into any of the four varnas. I do not know enough about the situation in south India. But in Northern India, castes such as Khatris and Kayasths are difficult to fit into the varna system. The Khatris are par excellence traders, but they are not classified amongst vaishyas. Nor are they part of the Kshatriyas.}}</ref>

The [[Saraswat Brahmin]]s are the [[purohit]]s of Khatris and accept gifts only from them.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Nalwa|first=Vanit|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ULhgNexD92QC&q=khatris+sarsut|title=Hari Singh Nalwa, "champion of the Khalsaji" (1791-1837)|date=13 January 2009|publisher=Manohar, New Delhi |isbn=978-81-7304-785-5|pages=97|language=en}}</ref> Jürgen Schaflechner cites the historian Rowe who states such Saraswat Brahmins who were considered a low caste, formed a symbiotic relationship with [[Vaishya]] castes such as [[Khatris]], [[Lohanas]], etc. who were trying to raise their varna status - which in turn would benefit the Saraswats as well. For this purpose, certain religious texts were written during the British Raj era.<ref>{{cite book |author1=Jürgen Schaflechner |date=2018 |title=Hinglaj Devi: Identity, Change, and Solidification at a Hindu Temple in Pakistan |publisher=Oxford University Press |pages=59–60 |isbn=978-0-19-085052-4 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=khBADwAAQBAJ&pg=PA59}}</ref>

[[Susan Bayly]] states the Khatris had scribal traditions and despite this Khatri caste organisations in the British Raj era tried to portray their caste as Kshatriyas. Similar caste glorifying ideas were written by the historian [[Baij Nath Puri|Puri]] who describes Khatris as "one of the most acute, energetic, and remarkable race [sic] in India", "pure descendants of the old Vedic Kshatriyas" and "true representatives of the Aryan nobility". Puri also tried to show the Khatris as higher than the Rajputs whose blood he considered "impure", mixed with ‘inferior’ Kolis or ‘aborigines’.<ref name="ma"/> She considers his views to represent those of "pre-Independence race theorists". Bayly further describes the Khatris as a "caste title of north Indians with military and scribal traditions".<ref>{{Cite book |last=Bayly |first=Susan |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HbAjKR_iHogC&dq=khatri+military+scribal+bayly&pg=PA385 |title=Caste, Society and Politics in India from the Eighteenth Century to the Modern Age |date=22 February 2001 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-521-79842-6 |pages=385 |language=en}}</ref> Hardip Singh Syan says Khatris considered themselves to be of pure [[Vedic]] descent and thus superior to the Rajputs, who like them claim the Kshatriya status of the Hindu [[Varna (Hinduism)|varna system]].<ref name="syan22"/>

=== Punjab ===
Historian Kenneth W. Jones states the Khatris of Punjab had some justification in claiming Kshatriya status from the British government. However, the fact that this claim was not granted at the time showing their ambiguous position in the varna system. Although Jones also classifies Khatris as one of the Vaishya caste of Punjabi Hindus, he shows that their social status was higher than the Arora, Suds and Baniyas in the 19th century Punjab. He quotes Ibbetson who states that the Punjabi Khatris who held prominent military and civil posts were traditionally different from the Aroras, Suds or Baniyas who were rural, of low status and mostly commercial. Punjabi Khatris, on the other hand, were urban, usually prosperous and literate. Thus, the Khatris led the Vaishyas in seeking a higher social position in the flexible Varna hierarchy based on their superior achievements. Similar social mobility efforts were followed by other Hindus in Punjab.<ref name="JonesJones19762" />

McLane also describes them as a "mercantile caste who claimed to be Kshatriyas". In the 19th century, British failed to agree whether their claim of Kshatriya status should be accepted. [[John Nesfield|Nesfield]] and [[George Campbell (civil servant)|Campbell]] were leaning towards accepting this claim but [[Herbert Hope Risley|Risley]] and [[Denzil Ibbetson|Ibbetson]] cast doubts on it. McLane opines that the confusion was caused since Khatris pursued mercantile occupations and not military ones. However, he adds that this Vaishya occupation fact was balanced by their origin myths, the "possible" derivation of the word Khatri from ''Kshatriya,'' their large physical stature, the superior status accorded to them by other [[Punjabis]] as well as the willingness of the [[Saraswat Brahmin]]s, their [[Purohit|chaplains]], to accept cooked food from them.<ref name="McLane2002222" />

In the case of [[Sikhs|Sikh]] Khatris, their Kshatriya claim reflects a contradictory attitude towards the traditional Hindu caste system. It is evident in ''[[Guru Granth Sahib]]'', which on the one hand rises above the Hindu caste paradigm and on the other hand seeks to portray the Khatri gurus as a group of warrior-defenders of their faith, just as with the Kshatriya varna.<ref name="dhavan2"/>

Majority of the male members of the [[Arya Samaj]] in the late 19th century Punjab came from the [[Arora]] and Khatri merchant castes. In Punjab, the Kshatriya castes who were ritually higher than the Aroras and Khatris had been disempowered and thus the Brahmins who had lost their patrons had to turn to these non-Kshatriya castes. [[Christophe Jaffrelot]] explains the attraction of these trading castes to the Arya Samaj as a means of social mobility associated with their prosperity during the British rule. He cites N. G. Barrier to show that the philosophy of the Arya Samaj founder, [[Dayananda Saraswati]], was responsible for the aspirations of these [[Vaishya]] castes from Punjab to higher status:<ref name="Jaffrelot2010">{{cite book |author=Christophe Jaffrelot |date=2010 |title=Religion, Caste, and Politics in India |publisher=Primus Books |pages=98– |isbn=9789380607047 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XAO3i_gS61wC&pg=PA98 |quote=In 1891, more than half the 9,105 male members of the movement belonged to the Khatri and Arora merchant castes. This sociological composition reflected the same socio-cultural logic as in Gujarat where Dayananda had set up the Arya samaj with the support of traders seeking a better status more in keeping with their new prosperity (Jordens 1978) linked with the economic advance of British India; in the Punjab, his movement developed along the same lines among the merchant castes which felt that they could aspire all the more legitimately to the leadership of their community as the Brahmins and Kshatriyas, who had been hierarchically superior to them had been marginalized. Barrier hence explains the attraction that the Arya Samaj exercised over the merchant castes by the fact that: Dayananda's claim that caste should be determined primarily by merit not birth, opened new paths of social mobility to educated Vaishyas who were trying to achieve social status commensurate with their improving economic status.}}</ref>

{{blockquote| Dayananda's claim that caste should be determined primarily by merit not birth, opened new paths of social mobility to educated [[Vaishyas]] who were trying to achieve social status commensurate with their improving economic status<ref name="Jaffrelot2010"/>}}

=== Rajasthan, Gujarat and Maharashtra ===
[[Dasharatha Sharma]] described Khatris of [[Rajasthan]] as a mixed ''pratiloma'' caste of low ritual status but they could be a mixed caste born of Kshatriya fathers and Brahmin mothers.<ref>{{cite book|last=Sharma|first=Dasharatha|title=Early Chauhān dynasties: a study of Chauhān political history, Chauhān political institutions, and life in the Chauhān dominions, from 800 to 1316 A.D.|publisher=Motilal Banarsidass|year=1975|page=279|author-link=Dasharatha Sharma}}</ref> [[Banking]], [[Trade|trading]], agriculture and service are traditional occupations of the Khatris in Rajasthan. The [[Literacy|literacy rate]] is appreciably high among them.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Singh|first=K.S|title=India's Communities A-G|publisher=OUP India |year=1998 |isbn=978-0195633542 |pages=1728}}</ref>

[[Ashok Malik]], former press secretary to the President of India, says that there were two groups of Khatris in Gujarat, that arrived right after the Mughal invasion and during the reign of [[Akbar]] respectively. The latter considered themselves superior to the former and they called themselves "Brahmakshatriyas" after arriving in Gujarat. When the older Khatri community of Gujarat started prospering, they also started calling themselves "Brahmakshatriya", causing the new Khatri community to panic and adopt the name "Nayar Brahmakshatriyas" for themselves. In addition, another community - the Gujarati [[Teli]]s, considered an [[Other Backward Class|Other Backward Class (OBC)]] in India began to call themselves ''Khatris.'' Malik calls this as [[Sanskritization]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Malik|first=Ashok|date=2010|title=Caste Census|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/23006464|journal=India International Centre Quarterly|volume=37|issue=1|pages=142–147|issn=0376-9771|jstor=23006464}}</ref>

Historian Vijaya Gupchup from the [[University of Mumbai]] states that in [[Maharashtra]], Brahmins showed resentment in the attempt by the [[Marathi people|Marathi]] Khatris or Koshti to elevate themselves from ritually low status to Kshatriya by taking advantage of the British neutrality towards castes. She quotes a translation from a [[Marathi language|Marathi]] publication that gave a Brahminic opinion of this attempt:

{{blockquote|Everyone does what he wants, Sonars have become Brahmins, Treemungalacharya was insulted by throwing cowdung at him in Pune, but he has no shame and still calls himself a Brahmin. Similarly a Khatri or Koshti who are included in Panchal at places other than Bombay, call themselves Kshatriya in Bombay and say their needles are the arrows and their thimbles are the sheaths. How surprising that those Sonars and Khatris at the hands of whom even Shudras will not take water have become Brahmins and Kshatriyas. In short day by day higher castes are disappearing and lower castes are prospering.<ref name="Gupchup1993">{{cite book|author=Vijaya V. Gupchup|title=Bombay: Social Change, 1813-1857|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=BeXZAAAAMAAJ |year=1993 |publisher=Popular Book Depot|page=191|quote=The cynical remarks of the Brahmin point out that there was a general tendency of the castes to elevate themselves in the social strata, no doubt taking advantage of the British policy of neutrality towards castes. Thus he says: Everyone does what he wants, Sonars have become Brahmins, Treemungalacharya was insulted by throwing cowdung at him in Pune, but he has no shame and still calls himself a Brahmin. Similarly a (Marathi) Khatri or Koshti (weavers) who are included in Panchal at places other than Bombay, call themselves Kshatriya in Bombay and say their needles are the arrows and their thimbles are the sheaths. How surprising that those Sonars and Khatris at the hands of whom even Sudras will not take water have become Brahmins and Kshatriyas. He continues, in short day by day higher castes are disappearing and lower castes are prospering.}}</ref>}}

==Religious groups==
===Hindu Khatris===
The vast majority of Khatris are Hindu.<ref name="hd"/> Many Hindu Khatris made their first newborn a Sikh. Daughters were married into both Hindu and Sikh families according to the Khatri sub-hierarchy rules.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Singh|first=Manpreet J.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=3KL8DwAAQBAJ&q=many+hindu+families+made+their+firstborn+a+sikh+manpreet+j+singh&pg=PT39|title=The Sikh Next Door: An Identity in Transition |date=31 August 2020|publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing|isbn=978-93-89165-58-6|language=en}}</ref> Hindu-Sikh intermarriages among Khatris and [[Arora]]s were common in the cities of [[Peshawar]] and [[Rawalpindi]].<ref>{{Cite book|last=Mooney|first=Nicola|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=k1B2vdLBizIC&dq=khatris+aroras+peshawar+rawalpindi&pg=PA12|title=Rural Nostalgias and Transnational Dreams: Identity and Modernity Among Jat Sikhs|date=1 January 2011 |publisher=University of Toronto Press|isbn=978-0-8020-9257-1|pages=12|language=en}}</ref> They worship [[Hinglaj Mata mandir|Hinglaj Mata]], [[Chandi|Chandi Mata]], [[Shiva]], [[Hanuman]] and [[Dashavatara|Vishnu's avatars]]. Worship of [[totem]]istic symbols such as snakes and trees used to be common among them. [[Meditation]] upon the flame while reciting [[Vindhyavasini|Vidhyavasini's]] hymns was a common practice and reverence was paid to the dead ancestors.<ref>{{Cite book|last1=Hertel|first1=Bradley R.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=r-OYL6Khg0UC&dq=khatris+worship&pg=PA199|title=Living Banaras: Hindu Religion in Cultural Context|last2=Humes|first2=Cynthia Ann|date=1 January 1993|publisher=SUNY Press|isbn=978-0-7914-1331-9|language=en}}</ref><ref name="sc">{{Cite book|last=Puri|first=Baij Nath|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=tXX1MAAACAAJ|title=The Khatris, a Socio-cultural Study |date=1988|publisher=M.N. Publishers and Distributors|pages=67–72, 149–150|language=en}}</ref> They are both vegetarian and non-vegetarian depending on their affiliations with the sects of [[Vaishnavism]] and [[Shaktism]] respectively.<ref>{{Cite book|last1=Nayar|first1=V. G.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=3sAn2eNvF7EC&dq=khatris+vegetarian&pg=PA124|title=Sociology of Religion in India|last2=Nayar|first2=M. G.|date=2001|publisher=Cosmo Publications |isbn=978-81-7755-151-8|pages=124|language=en}}</ref> Sects of [[Arya Samaj]], [[Nirankari]] and [[Radha Soami|Radhasoami]] are also followed.<ref name="sc"/>

===Sikh Khatris===
All the ten Sikh Gurus were from various Khatri clans.<ref>{{cite book|last=Singha|first=H. S.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gqIbJz7vMn0C&pg=PA125 |title=The Encyclopedia of Sikhism|publisher=Hemkunt Press|year=2000|isbn=978-81-7010-301-1|page=125}}</ref> The early followers of [[Guru Nanak]] were Khatris but later a large number of [[Jat]]s joined the faith.<ref>{{cite book|author=Richard M. Eaton|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aIF6DwAAQBAJ&pg=PP168|title=India in the Persianate Age: 1000-1765 |publisher=Penguin |year=2019 |isbn=9780141966557 |pages=168–169 |quote=The Sikh community grew rapidly in the sixteenth century. Nanak's earliest followers had been fellow Khatris engaged in petty trade, shopkeeping, or lower level civil service in the Lodi or Mughal bureaucracies. But as the movement grew, it experienced a significant influx of Jat cultivators.}}</ref> Khatris and Brahmins opposed "the demand that the Sikhs set aside the distinctive customs of their castes and families, including the older rituals."<ref>{{cite book |last=Dhavan |first=Purnima |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=q0ZpAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA42|title=When Sparrows Became Hawks: The Making of the Sikh Warrior Tradition, 1699-1799|publisher=Oxford University Press|year=2011|isbn=978-0-19987-717-1|pages=42, 47, 184}}</ref>

[[Bhapa]] (pronounced as Pahpa) is a term used in a derogatory sense to denote Sikhs who left [[Pothohar Plateau|Potohar Region]] of modern-day Pakistan during [[Partition of India|Partition]], specifically of Khatri and [[Arora]] caste. Bhapa translates to elder brother in the [[Pahari-Pothwari|Potohari]] dialect spoken around [[Rawalpindi District|Rawalpindi]] region. [[W. H. McLeod|McLeod]], referring to the Khatris and Aroras says "The term is typically used dismissively by [[Jat Sikh|Jats]] to express opprobrium towards Sikhs of these castes. Until recently it was never used in polite company or print, but today the word is used quite openly"<ref name="ds"/><ref>{{Cite news|last=Singh|first=Pukhraj|date=31 May 2014|title=Bluestar Baby Boomers |work=Newslaundary |url=https://www.newslaundry.com/2014/05/31/bluestar-baby-boomers|access-date=25 August 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Kumar |first=Dharminder|date=3 January 2016|title=The Sardar Joke Is On You|work=Mumbai Mirror|url=http://www.mumbaimirror.com/columns/columnists/by-invitation/The-Sardar-joke-is-on-you/articleshow/50422145.cms|access-date=25 August 2021}}</ref> According to Birinder Pal Singh, [[Jat Sikh]]s consider only themselves as Sikhs and consider Khatris as "bhapas".<ref>{{Cite book|last=Singh|first=Birinder Pal|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=be1GDwAAQBAJ&q=khatris|title=Sikhs in the Deccan and North-East India|date=12 January 2018|publisher=Taylor & Francis|isbn=978-1-351-20105-6|language=en}}</ref> In Nicola Mooney's opinion, Jat Sikhs consider Arora Sikhs as "Hindu Punjabis" which reserves Sikhism for the Jats alone, denying even the fully [[Amrit Sanskar|baptised]] Arora as Sikhs.<ref name="js"/>

=== Muslim Khatris ===
{{main|Muslim Khatris}}

According to Historian [[Baij Nath Puri|B. N. Puri]], [[Muslims|Muslim]] Khatris are commonly known as [[Khawaja Shaikh|Khojas]] in Punjab.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Puri|first=Baij Nath|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=tXX1MAAACAAJ|title=The Khatris, a Socio-cultural Study |date=1988 |publisher=M.N. Publishers and Distributors|pages=149–150|language=en}}</ref> [[Khattak]] tribe of [[Pashtuns]] is credited with origin from the Khatris but was divided in belief to its descent according to the 1883 book "Glossary of the Tribes and Castes of the Punjab and North-West Frontier Province".<ref>{{Cite book|last=Nalwa|first=Vanit|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ULhgNexD92QC&dq=khattak+khatris&pg=PA360|title=Hari Singh Nalwa, "champion of the Khalsaji" (1791-1837)|date=13 January 2009|publisher=Manohar, New Delhi|isbn=978-81-7304-785-5|pages=98|language=en}}</ref>

== Culture and lifestyle ==
According to [[Prakash Tandon]], during Khatri weddings, a ritual is carried out to test the Khatri groom's strength. The groom is supposed to slice the thick branch or stem of a [[Prosopis cineraria|Jandi Tree]] (Prosopis cineraria) in one blow using a sword.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Tandon |first=Prakash |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=p6zTk-ibjTwC&q=punjabi+khatri+history |title=Punjabi Century, 1857-1947 |date=1968 |publisher=University of California Press |isbn=978-0-520-01253-0 |pages=136 |language=en}}</ref>{{Better source needed|reason=Tandon was a businessman, see [[WP:HISTRS]]|date=August 2021}} During the pregnancy period of a female, a [[baby shower]] ceremony called "reetan" or "goadbharai" is carried out amongst Khatris and Aroras. During the event, gifts are showered to the pregnant mother from family and friends among other traditions.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Rait |first=S. K. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=hRfkTq1ykSIC&q=born+in+a+khatri+family&pg=PA71 |title=Sikh Women in England: Their Religious and Cultural Beliefs and Social Practices |date=2005 |publisher=Trentham Books |isbn=978-1-85856-353-4 |pages=71 |language=en}}</ref>

== Literature and in popular culture ==
Khatris are mentioned in a popular Punjabi literature "[[Heer Ranjha]]" written by [[Waris Shah]].

<blockquote>Heer's beauty slays rich [[Khwaja|Khojas]] and Khatris in the [[bazaar]], like a murderous [[Qizilbash|Kizilbash]] trooper riding out of the royal camp armed with a sword
:— Waris Shah (Translated by Charles Frederick Usborne)<ref>{{Cite book|last=Shāh|first=Vāris̲|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Exo2AAAAMAAJ&q=the+adventures+of+hir+and+ranjha+khatri|title=The Adventures of Hir & Ranjha|date=1966|publisher=Lion Art Press|pages=41|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Shah|first=Waris|url=http://crossasia-repository.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/246/1/hir.pdf|title=The Adventure of Hir and Ranjha |publisher=Rupa |year=2003 |isbn=978-8129103796|translator-last=Usborne|translator-first=Charles Frederick}}</ref></blockquote>

== Sikh theology ==

According to ''[[Bichitra Natak]]'', traditionally said to be the autobiography of the last Sikh Guru, [[Gobind Singh]], but possibly not so,<ref>The Sikh Struggle in the Eighteenth Century and Its Relevance for Today, W. H. McLeod, History of Religions, Vol. 31, No. 4, Sikh Studies (May 1992), pp. 344-362, The University of Chicago Press/ quote: "Although Bachitar Natak is traditionally attributed to Guru Gobind Singh, there is a strong case to be made for regarding it as the work of one of his followers..."</ref> the Bedi sub-caste of the Khatris derives its lineage from [[Kusha (Ramayana)|Kush]], the son of [[Rama]] (according to Hindu epic ''[[Ramayana]]''). Similarly, according to the same legend, the [[Sodhi]] sub-caste claims descent from [[Lava (Ramayana)|Lav]], the other son of Rama.<ref name="Bichitra2">The Cosmic Drama: Bichitra Natak, Author Gobind Singh, Publisher Himalayan International Institute of Yoga Science and Philosophy of the U.S.A., 1989 {{ISBN|0-89389-116-9}}, {{ISBN|978-0-89389-116-9}}</ref>{{Better source needed|reason=not [[WP:HSC]]|date=August 2021}}

In [[Guru Granth Sahib]], the primary scripture of [[Sikhism]], Khatri is mentioned as one among the four [[Varna (Hinduism)|varnas]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Singh|first=Nikky-Guninder Kaur|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8daLDwAAQBAJ&dq=khatri+brahmin+shudra+vaishya+guru+nanak&pg=PT62 |title=Sikhism: An Introduction|date=22 February 2011|publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing|isbn=978-0-85773-549-2|language=en}}</ref>

{{Blockquote|text=ਖਤ੍ਰੀ ਬ੍ਰਾਹਮਣ ਸੂਦ ਵੈਸ ਉਪਦੇਸੁ ਚਹੁ ਵਰਨਾ ਕਉ ਸਾਝਾ ॥ (SGGS, ang 747)
''Khatri brahman sud vais updesu cahu varna ku sanjha''
[[Kshatriya]]s, [[Brahmin]]s, [[Shudra]]s and [[Vaishya]]s all have the same mandate
|multiline=yes
}}

[[File:Portret van een onbekende handelaar uit Hazara Khutree. Hindoo trader. Hazara (titel op object), RP-F-2001-7-1122E-20.jpg|thumb|Photograph of a Hindu Khatri man of [[Hazara region|Hazara]] c. 1868-1872|188x188px]]

[[Guru Gobind Singh]], said the following in a [[Savaiya|swayya]]:

{{Blockquote|text=''Chattri ko poot ho, Baman ko naheen kayee tap aavat ha jo karon; Ar aur janjaar jito greh ko tohe tyaag, kahan chit taan mai dharon, Ab reejh ke deh vahey humko jo-oo, hau binti kar jor karoon; Jab aao ki audh nidaan bane, att hi ran main tab jujh maroon.''
I am the son of a Chhatri (Khatri), not of a [[Brahmin]] and I will live according to my [[Dharma]]. All other complications of life are meaningless for me, and I set my heart on the path of righteousness. I humbly beseech thee God Almighty that when the time comes for me to fulfill my Dharma, may I die with honour in the field of battle.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Nalwa|first=Vanit|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ULhgNexD92QC&q=chhatri+ko+poot |title=Hari Singh Nalwa, "champion of the Khalsaji" (1791-1837) |date=2009-01-13 |publisher=Manohar, New Delhi |isbn=978-81-7304-785-5 |pages=329 |language=en}}</ref>
|multiline=yes
|author=Translated by Vanit Nalwa
}}

== Related communities ==
=== Arora ===
The [[Arora]] is a community that Levi describes as a sub-caste of Khatris.<ref name="id"/> They originate in [[Punjab]] and [[Sindh]] region. The name is derived from their native place [[Aror]] and the community comprises both Hindus and Sikhs.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hans |first=Patrick |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=0AyDDQAAQBAJ&q=Aror |title=The Oxford Dictionary of Family Names in Britain and Ireland |year=2016 |isbn=9780192527479 |page=78|publisher=Oxford University Press }}</ref> As per [[W. H. McLeod]], a historian of Sikhism, "traditionally the Aroras, though a relatively high caste were inferior to the Khatris, but the difference has now progressively narrowed. Khatri-Arora marriages are not unknown nowadays."<ref name="McLeod_AtoZ"/>

=== Lohana, Bhatia, and Bhanushali ===
According to Claude Markovits, castes such as [[Bhatia caste|Bhatia]] and [[Lohana]] were close to the Khatris and intermarried with them.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Markovits|first=Claude|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=M2vu7Odjz6kC&q=claude+markovits+india+and+the+world+2000|title=The Global World of Indian Merchants, 1750–1947: Traders of Sind from Bukhara to Panama|date=22 June 2000|publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-1-139-43127-9 |pages=47 |language=en}}</ref> Jürgen Schaflechner mentions that many Khatris and [[Bhatia caste|Bhatias]] were absorbed into Lohanas when they arrived in Sindh during the 18th century from cities in Punjab such as [[Multan]].<ref name="tp">{{Cite book |last=Schaflechner |first=Jürgen |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=khBADwAAQBAJ&q=schaflechner+hinglaj+devi|title=Hinglaj Devi: Identity, Change, and Solidification at a Hindu Temple in Pakistan|date=2018|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-085052-4|pages=73–74|language=en}}</ref> He further adds that the genealogy of communities such as Khatri, Lohana and Arora is described in the composition of Hiṃgulā Purāṇ that brings them all into one mytho-historic narrative. He also notes that common mythologies found among Khatris and Lohanas. Some members, around 10-15% of the [[Lohana|Sindhi Lohanas]] began working for the local rulers and hence achieved a higher status than Khatris and Lohanas. These people came to known as "[[Amils]]" while the ones who continued with their merchant professions came to be known as "[[Bhaiband]]s". The Amils then started to recruit members from the general Khatris and Lohanas.<ref name="tp"/>

Upendra Thakur mentions that there is a strong connection between the Khatris, Aroras, Lohanas and the [[Bhanushali]]s who all recruit the Saraswat Brahmins as their priests.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Thakur|first=Upendra|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wubpGAAACAAJ|title=Sindhi Culture |date=1997 |publisher=Sindhi Academy|isbn=978-81-87096-02-3|pages=61|language=en}}</ref>

=== Gaddi ===
Gaddi is a [[nomad]]ic [[Sheepherding|shepherding]] [[tribe]] that resides in the mountainous terrains of the [[Himalayas]]. Gaddi is an amalgamation of various groups such as Khatris, Rajputs, Brahmins etc.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Sharma|first=Manorma|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=CDxa5G2S0AwC&q=gaddi+khatri&pg=PA10|title=Tribal Melodies of Himachal Pradesh: Gaddi folk music|date=1998|publisher=APH Publishing |isbn=978-81-7024-912-2 |pages=9 |language=en}}</ref> Most [[Gaddis]] of Himachal Pradesh call themselves Khatris.<ref name="tc"/> There is a popular saying among them "Ujreya Lahore te baseya Bharmaur" meaning that when [[Lahore]] was deserted (possibly by the Muslim invasion), [[Bharmour]] was inhabited. Some Khatris clans are known to have settled there during Aurangzeb's reign.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hāṇḍā |first=Omacanda |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2EaFNQ7Zfl8C&dq=Ujreya+Lahore+te+baseya+Bharmaur&pg=PA29 |title=Gaddi Land in Chamba: Its History, Art & Culture: New Light on the Early Wooden Temples |date=2005 |publisher=Indus Publishing |isbn=978-81-7387-174-0 |pages=29 |language=en}}</ref>

